$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{x-3}{2-\sqrt{x+1}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{x-3}{2-\sqrt{x+1}}\cdot\dfrac{2+\sqrt{x+1}}{2+\sqrt{x+1}}\qquad\gray{\text{Rationalize the denominator}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(x-3)(2+\sqrt{x+1})}{2^2-(x+1)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{(x-3)}(2+\sqrt{x+1})}{-1\cancel{(x-3)}}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel out common factors}}
\\\\
&=-2-\sqrt{x+1} \text{, for }x\neq 3
\end{align}$

This means that the two expressions have the same value for all $x$-values (in their domains) except for $3$.

We can now use the following theorem:

If $f(x)=g(x)$ for all $x$-values in a given interval except for $x=c$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to c}f(x)=\lim_{x\to c}g(x)$.
